<interrogationes unitas="imago_opus">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I resize an image to an exact width and height?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_scalare ImagoScalaModus">Call
      imago_scalare(fons, latitudo, altitudo, modus, piscina). It
      returns a NEW Imago whose RGBA pixels live in the piscina you
      pass; the source is not touched. A NIHIL source, NIHIL pixels or
      a zero dimension give an empty Imago (pixela NIHIL, 0 x 0), so
      test .pixela before using the result. The source's own size is
      not checked, so do not pass a source with pixels but a zero
      width or height. An unknown modus falls back to nearest
      neighbour.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I choose between PROXIMUS, BILINEARIS and AREA?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_scalare ImagoScalaModus" via="lib/pictor_documentum.c">Use
      IMAGO_SCALA_AREA to shrink: it averages every source pixel under
      each output pixel, weighted by alpha. Bilinear reads only a 2 x 2
      neighbourhood whatever the scale, so 1024 to 16 would sample 4
      of 4096 pixels. Use BILINEARIS to enlarge smoothly (it is exact
      at 1:1) and PROXIMUS for fast or pixel-art scaling.
      pictor_documentum picks AREA when the new width is smaller,
      bilinear otherwise.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is AREA scaling alpha-weighted, and why does a transparent pixel not tint its neighbours?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_scalare Imago" via="lib/imago_opus.worklog.md include/imago_typus.h">Imago
      pixels are RGBA and NOT premultiplied. A plain average would let
      invisible pixels pull their colour into the result (the halo
      around icon edges). AREA therefore averages alpha by coverage and
      averages RGB weighted by alpha times coverage, rounding half up:
      opaque red beside transparent white at 2:1 gives (255, 0, 0,
      128). A block that is fully transparent comes out as all
      zeros.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I rely on imago_scalare_ad_limites filling the limit exactly in one dimension?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_scalare_ad_limites imago_extrahere_et_scalare imago_scalare" via="lib/imago_opus.worklog.md">No.
      The fit-within ratio is computed in 16-bit fixed point and
      truncated twice, so the side that should touch the limit is
      often one pixel short (a 1000 px crop asked for 16 gives 15; a 3
      x 3 image fitted to 100 x 100 gives 99 x 99), and then the whole
      image is resampled. It also always scales, enlarging small
      images too. When the size must be exact, compute it yourself and
      call imago_scalare, as icones and pictor_documentum do.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I crop an image without resampling it?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_excidere imago_extrahere_et_scalare" via="lib/specimen.c">Use
      imago_excidere(fons, x, y, latitudo, altitudo, piscina): it
      copies the intersection of the region with the source, pixel for
      pixel, into a new Imago (a 5 x 5 region at (3, 3) of a 4 x 4
      image gives 1 x 1). A region wholly outside gives an empty
      Imago. imago_extrahere_et_scalare always runs a resampler over
      the region (and fits it within limits), which is the reason
      specimen gives for cropping by hand.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I alpha-blend one image onto another with imago_transcribere?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_transcribere imago_creare">No:
      it is a copy, not a composite. Destination pixels are REPLACED,
      alpha included, even where the source is fully transparent.
      dx and dy may be negative and every edge is clipped; an image
      wholly outside changes nothing. It uses memcpy per row, so do not
      pass the same Imago as source and destination (overlapping rows).
      Start a canvas with imago_creare, which fills one RGBA
      colour.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I draw a rectangle outline or a filled rectangle into an Imago?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_rectangulum">imago_rectangulum(dest,
      x, y, latitudo, altitudo, crassitudo, r, g, b, a) draws the
      outline with its thickness INSIDE [x, x + latitudo) x [y, y +
      altitudo), clipped to the image; x and y may be negative.
      0 draws nothing. A thickness from half the shorter side (rounded
      up) up to the shorter side fills it. Never go above the shorter
      side, whatever the header says: the bands are not clamped to the
      rectangle, so a thicker value paints outside it (w 4, h 20,
      thickness 5 paints columns x - 1 and x + 4).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can these functions handle very large images?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_scalare imago_scalare_ad_limites imago_creare">Only
      moderately. The AREA path computes in s64, but the nearest and
      bilinear paths index the source in s32 and wrap near a 23170 x
      23170 source; buffer sizes are latitudo * altitudo * 4 in i32,
      which is UNSIGNED and wraps above 4 GB; and the fit-within paths
      multiply the limit by 65536 in s32, so limits must stay below
      32768.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
